it's reference equality. `{ a: 1 }` creates a brand new object every time the parent renders, so `prevProps.style !== nextProps.style` even though the contents are identical. react compares props with `Object.is`, which doesn't look inside objects.

also, if the child isn't wrapped in `React.memo`, it re-renders whenever the parent does, no matter what props you pass. so the object literal only matters once you've memoized the child.

fixes, pick one:

```jsx
const options = { dense: true };

function Parent() {
  return <Child options={options} />;
}
```

hoisting works when the object is static. if it depends on state or props:

```jsx
function Parent({ size }) {
  const options = useMemo(() => ({ dense: true, size }), [size]);
  return <Child options={options} />;
}
```

or just pass primitives (`dense={true} size={size}`) so the shallow compare works on its own.

same thing applies to inline arrays and functions (`onClick={() => ...}`), those are new references each render too.

if you're on react 19 with the react compiler enabled, it memoizes this stuff automatically, so you'd only hit this without it or when it bails out on a component.

fwiw it's not always worth fixing. a re-render that's cheap is fine, only bother when the child is expensive or you've seen it in the profiler.

